<question no="6613" partialPoint="true" point="50" skillType="1" type="Insertion">
	<title><![CDATA[Insertion ]]></title>
	<direction><![CDATA[パッセージ中の空所を最も適当と思われる文で埋めなさい。]]></direction>
	<questionText><![CDATA[The ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Rome is one of the wonders of art.  The whole space, 930 square meters in all, is covered with marvelous paintings.  There are 343 figures, most of them two or three times life-size.  The artist was the great Michelangelo.

Michelangelo Buonarroti was born near the city of Caprese in Italy.  His father was very proud of belonging to one of the oldest families of Florence.  He wished young Michelangelo could become a scholar, but the boy was interested only in sculpture.  [He knew, however, that he could never be a great sculptor without knowing how to draw.]

When he was 13, Michelangelo entered the workshop of the painter of Ghirlandajo, in Florence. Michelangelo went with other boys to copy the painting of Ghirlandajo.  He made fun of the work of some of the other boys.  One of them in a fit of anger broke Michelangelo's nose.  It remained crooked all his life.

Michelangelo never found it easy to get along with people.  [He had many quarrels with Popes and rulers.]  He made a great deal of money but he spent it all on his family.

[There has never been a greater sculptor than Michelangelo.]  He was an architect and an engineer too.  He designed the dome of St. Peter's in Rome.  And at one time he was the chief engineer of all the defenses of Florence.

Michelangelo made himself famous as a sculptor by his "David".  A great block of marble had stood in Florence for a century.  Another sculptor had abandoned it.  From the block Michelangelo carved the statue of David, the most famous statue in the world.

When the Pope asked Michelangelo to paint the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel, the artist refused.  [He did not wish to spend his time painting.]  But the Pope insisted, and Michelangelo at last agreed.  For four years he worked on the ceiling.  He had to either lie flat on his back on a high scaffold or sit with his neck bent far backward.  In summer the air near the ceiling was terrifically hot; in winter it was freezing cold.  [But the artist worked hour after hour.]  When the Pope told him to hurry he even moved his bed into the chapel. He slept in it at night.

Michelangelo lived without even ordinary comforts, but lived to be 90.]]></questionText>

	<description/>

</question>




                                <combinationQuestion no="2248" type="readingComprehension">
                                    <title><![CDATA[イタリアの彫刻家ミケランジェロ]]></title>
                                    <direction><![CDATA[次のパッセージを読んで、質問に答えなさい。]]></direction>


                                            <passage><![CDATA[The ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Rome is one of the wonders of art.  The whole space, 930 square meters in all, is covered with marvelous paintings.  There are 343 figures, most of them two or three times life-size.  The artist was the great Michelangelo.<br/><br/>Michelangelo Buonarroti was born near the city of Caprese in Italy.  His father was very proud of belonging to one of the oldest families of Florence.  He wished young Michelangelo could become a scholar, but the boy was interested only in sculpture.  He knew, however, that he could never be a great sculptor without knowing how to draw.<br/><br/>When he was 13, Michelangelo entered the workshop of the painter of Ghirlandajo, in Florence. Michelangelo went with other boys to copy the painting of Ghirlandajo.  He made fun of the work of some of the other boys.  One of them in a fit of anger broke Michelangelo's nose.  It remained crooked all his life.<br/><br/>Michelangelo never found it easy to get along with people.  He had many quarrels with Popes and rulers.  He made a great deal of money but he spent it all on his family.<br/><br/>There has never been a greater sculptor than Michelangelo.  He was an architect and an engineer too.  He designed the dome of St. Peter's in Rome.  And at one time he was the chief engineer of all the defenses of Florence.<br/><br/>Michelangelo made himself famous as a sculptor by his "David".  A great block of marble had stood in Florence for a century.  Another sculptor had abandoned it.  From the block Michelangelo carved the statue of David, the most famous statue in the world.<br/><br/>When the Pope asked Michelangelo to paint the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel, the artist refused.  He did not wish to spend his time painting.  But the Pope insisted, and Michelangelo at last agreed.  For four years he worked on the ceiling.  He had to either lie flat on his back on a high scaffold or sit with his neck bent far backward.  In summer the air near the ceiling was terrifically hot; in winter it was freezing cold.  But the artist worked hour after hour.  When the Pope told him to hurry he even moved his bed into the chapel. He slept in it at night.<br/><br/>Michelangelo lived without even ordinary comforts, but lived to be 90.]]></passage>


                                            <en_script_sentence><![CDATA[***The ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Rome is one of the wonders of art.*** ***The whole space, 930 square meters in all, is covered with marvelous paintings.*** ***There are 343 figures, most of them two or three times life-size.*** ***The artist was the great Michelangelo.*** ***Michelangelo Buonarroti was born near the city of Caprese in Italy.*** ***His father was very proud of belonging to one of the oldest families of Florence.*** ***He wished young Michelangelo could become a scholar, but the boy was interested only in sculpture.*** ***He knew, however, that he could never be a great sculptor without knowing how to draw.***

***When he was 13, Michelangelo entered the workshop of the painter of Ghirlandajo, in Florence.*** ***Michelangelo went with other boys to copy the painting of Ghirlandajo.*** ***He made fun of the work of some of the other boys.*** ***One of them in a fit of anger broke Michelangelo's nose.*** ***It remained crooked all his life.***

***Michelangelo never found it easy to get along with people.*** ***He had many quarrels with Popes and rulers.*** ***He made a great deal of money but he spent it all on his family.***

***There has never been a greater sculptor than Michelangelo.*** ***He was an architect and an engineer too.*** ***He designed the dome of St. Peter's in Rome.*** ***And at one time he was the chief engineer of all the defenses of Florence.***

***Michelangelo made himself famous as a sculptor by his "David".*** ***A great block of marble had stood in Florence for a century.*** ***Another sculptor had abandoned it.*** ***From the block Michelangelo carved the statue of David, the most famous statue in the world.***

***When the Pope asked Michelangelo to paint the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel, the artist refused.*** ***He did not wish to spend his time painting.*** ***But the Pope insisted, and Michelangelo at last agreed.*** ***For four years he worked on the ceiling.*** ***He had to either lie flat on his back on a high scaffold or sit with his neck bent far backward.*** ***In summer the air near the ceiling was terrifically hot; in winter it was freezing cold.*** ***But the artist worked hour after hour.*** ***When the Pope told him to hurry he even moved his bed into the chapel.*** ***He slept in it at night.***

***Michelangelo lived without even ordinary comforts, but lived to be 90.***]]></en_script_sentence>

                                        <jp_script><![CDATA[　ローマのシスティナ礼拝堂の天井は芸術の驚異のひとつである。全空間は、合計９３０平方メートルで、驚くべき絵画で覆われている。３４３の肖像があり、それらのほとんどは、実物大の２倍または３倍である。その芸術家は偉大なミケランジェロだった。<br/><br/>　ミケランジェロ・ブオナローティは、イタリアのカプリーズという都市の近くで生まれた。彼の父親は、フィレンツェの中で最も古い家族のうちの一員であることに非常に誇りを感じていた。彼は、幼いミケランジェロが学者になれればよいと思っていた、しかし、その少年は彫刻にだけ興味を持っていた。しかしながら、彼は、描き方を知らずにすばらしい彫刻家になることは、決してできないとわかっていた。<br/><br/>　ミケランジェロは、１３歳だったとき、フィレンツェのギルランダイオという画家の工房に入った。ミケランジェロは、他の少年たちとギルランダイオの絵画を模写しに通った。彼は、その他の少年たちの何人かの作品を物笑いの種にした。彼らのうちのひとりは、かっとなってミケランジェロの鼻をへし折った。それは終生曲がったままだった。<br/><br/>　ミケランジェロは、人々とうまくやっていくことを簡単なことだと思ったことは決してなかった。彼は、教皇や支配者と多くの争いをした。彼は多くの財産を築き上げたが、それを彼の家族のためにすべて使った。<br/><br/>　ミケランジェロより偉大な彫刻家は、今までに一度も存在したことはない。彼は建築家であり、技師でもあった。彼は、ローマのサン・ピエトロ寺院のドームを設計した。そして彼は、ひと頃は、フィレンツェの防衛すべてを担う技師長だった。<br/><br/>　ミケランジェロは彼の「ダビデ像」によって、自分自身を彫刻家として有名にした。１世紀の間、フィレンツェに大きな大理石の塊が立っていた。別の彫刻家はそれを断念していた。その塊から、ミケランジェロは世界で最も有名な彫像である、ダビデの彫像を刻んだ。<br/><br/>　教皇がミケランジェロにシスティナ礼拝堂の天井に絵を描くように頼んだとき、その芸術家は拒否した。彼は、絵を描くことに自分の時間を使うことを望まなかった。しかし、教皇が強要したので、ついにミケランジェロは同意した。４年間、彼はその天井に取り組んだ。彼は、高い足場に仰向けに寝そべるか、首をはるか後ろへ曲げて座るかのどちらかをしなければならなかった。夏には、天井付近の空気は、ものすごく暑くなり、冬には、凍るような寒さだった。しかし、その芸術家は何時間も描いた。教皇が彼に急ぐよう言ったとき、彼はその礼拝堂に自分の寝床を移すことさえした。彼は、夜にはそこで眠った。<br/><br/>　ミケランジェロは平凡な楽しみさえなく暮らしていたが、９０歳まで生きた。]]></jp_script>


<question no="30165411" point="10" skillType="1" type="trueFalse">
	<title><![CDATA[Q & A True-False]]></title>
	<direction><![CDATA[次に述べられていることが正しければTrue、間違っていればFalseを選びなさい。]]></direction>
	<questionText><![CDATA[The person who painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel was Michelangelo.]]></questionText>

	<description><![CDATA[　システィナ礼拝堂の天井に絵を描いた人は、ミケランジェロだった。<br><br>解答：true<br><br>　　第１段落をよく読んでください。システィナ礼拝堂の天井の絵画のことについて述べられていることがわかるでしょう。そして、最後の文に "The artist was the great Michelangelo." と書いてあります。つまり、その絵画を描いたのはミケランジェロです。したがって、答えは true となります。]]></description>
	<answers><answer>1</answer></answers>
		<!-- 選択肢 -->



		<choices shuffleChoices="false">


                    <choice no="1"><![CDATA[True]]></choice>


                    <choice no="2"><![CDATA[False]]></choice>

		</choices>

</question>


<question no="30165511" point="10" skillType="1" type="trueFalse">
	<title><![CDATA[Q & A True-False]]></title>
	<direction><![CDATA[次に述べられていることが正しければTrue、間違っていればFalseを選びなさい。]]></direction>
	<questionText><![CDATA[Michelangelo was easy to get on with.]]></questionText>

	<description><![CDATA[　ミケランジェロは、うまくつきあうのが容易（な人物）だった。<br><br>解答：false<br><br>　　第４段落の最初の文を見てください。"Michelangelo never found it easy to get along with people." と書いてあります。この場合、"it" は "to" 以下を指す形式目的語です。つまり、ミケランジェロは人々とうまくやっていくということを簡単には思っていなかった訳です。したがって、答えは　false　です。ちなみに、問題文の "get on with　～" とは "get along with　～" と同じ「～とうまくやっていく」という意味です。]]></description>
	<answers><answer>2</answer></answers>
		<!-- 選択肢 -->



		<choices shuffleChoices="false">


                    <choice no="1"><![CDATA[True]]></choice>


                    <choice no="2"><![CDATA[False]]></choice>

		</choices>

</question>


